WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:03.003
All right, bladesmiths,
congratulations.

00:00:03.103 --> 00:00:04.370
You've made it to the second
round of this competition,

00:00:04.471 --> 00:00:06.205
and now it's time to take
those tempered blades

00:00:06.272 --> 00:00:08.941
and make them fully functional
by attaching handles to them.

00:00:09.041 --> 00:00:11.444
A lot of weapons are
defined by their handle.

00:00:11.511 --> 00:00:12.245
Good luck, bladesmiths.

00:00:12.312 --> 00:00:16.249
Your two hours starts now.

00:00:16.316 --> 00:00:17.050
All right.

00:00:17.116 --> 00:00:19.351
These guys are off to the races.

00:00:19.418 --> 00:00:21.220
I came in with an edge on,
but that edge doesn't matter.

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:23.356
I've got to remove all that
mild steel can that was left,

00:00:23.423 --> 00:00:26.759
and I've got to get this down to
a good solid carbon steel edge.

00:00:26.859 --> 00:00:28.193
Matt's shaping the handle.

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:30.329
He's already got the
pommel and guard on there.

00:00:30.396 --> 00:00:31.530
Yeah.

00:00:31.630 --> 00:00:33.166
When you make a knife for
this kind of competition,

00:00:33.232 --> 00:00:35.067
everyone pays
attention to the blade

00:00:35.167 --> 00:00:36.603
because that's where
it's going to break,

00:00:36.669 --> 00:00:37.937
that's where it's going to snap.

00:00:38.004 --> 00:00:39.372
You've got to make a
strong blade, sharp blade.

00:00:39.439 --> 00:00:43.409
Everything in their mind
is from the guard forward.

00:00:43.509 --> 00:00:45.779
I pick antler because
there's an outside layer

00:00:45.845 --> 00:00:47.947
that's extremely
tough, extremely hard.

00:00:48.047 --> 00:00:49.515
He's going to grind
right into the pith.

00:00:49.582 --> 00:00:50.683 line:14%
Right into the pith.

00:00:50.784 --> 00:00:52.451 line:8%
And the pith is just
like bony, spongy material.

00:00:52.552 --> 00:00:53.486
It's not strong.

00:00:53.553 --> 00:00:55.188
It looks like a sponge.

00:00:55.288 --> 00:00:56.289
Yeah.

00:00:56.355 --> 00:00:57.790
Everything seems to
be going great, and then

00:00:57.890 --> 00:00:59.492
all of a sudden, there it is.

00:00:59.592 --> 00:01:01.093
I got into the soft
part of the antler.

00:01:01.194 --> 00:01:02.595
What the crap am
I going to do now?

00:01:02.662 --> 00:01:04.163
If I start another
handle, there's no way

00:01:04.229 --> 00:01:05.397
I'm going to be able to finish.

00:01:05.497 --> 00:01:06.966
So I've just got to
roll with it and figure

00:01:07.033 --> 00:01:08.568
out a way to address this.

00:01:08.634 --> 00:01:10.904
My best bet is to fill
it as full of epoxy

00:01:11.004 --> 00:01:13.172
as I could get and then
coat the entire outside.

00:01:13.238 --> 00:01:17.176
The handle often decides
what that weapon is.

00:01:17.242 --> 00:01:19.044
Now, how thick of a
coat do you put on this?

00:01:19.145 --> 00:01:21.581
In something like this,
to get it 1/16 of an inch

00:01:21.647 --> 00:01:22.648
in is pretty good.

00:01:22.715 --> 00:01:24.050
Yeah.

00:01:24.117 --> 00:01:26.218
Now, Mark had a pretty
complete blade with an edge

00:01:26.318 --> 00:01:27.487
at the end of the first round.

00:01:27.553 --> 00:01:30.289
But he had a weird
kick to his tang.

00:01:30.356 --> 00:01:32.192
As I'm grinding
this tang, I just

00:01:32.292 --> 00:01:35.028
need to make sure that there's
enough meat left in that tang

00:01:35.095 --> 00:01:36.929
and handle to where it
makes for a strong knife.

00:01:36.996 --> 00:01:38.865
So the handle
design dictates how

00:01:38.931 --> 00:01:40.132
you're going to fight with it.

00:01:40.232 --> 00:01:41.667
That's a big heavy
blade hanging out there,

00:01:41.768 --> 00:01:43.870
and I don't want it coming
apart with the judges

00:01:43.936 --> 00:01:45.137
trying to do their tests on it.

00:01:45.204 --> 00:01:47.206
I know I've learned
a lot about handle

00:01:47.272 --> 00:01:49.809
making by destroying
other people's handles.

00:01:49.876 --> 00:01:51.477
[laughter]

00:01:51.544 --> 00:01:53.012
I've got everything
ready to fit on.

00:01:53.079 --> 00:01:54.614
I'm ready to epoxy
this thing together.

00:01:54.714 --> 00:01:58.050
And I'm screwing this butt cap
on, and I go to tighten it up--

00:01:58.151 --> 00:01:59.685
Ah.
Ah, ah.

00:01:59.785 --> 00:02:00.753
Wait, wait, wait.

00:02:00.820 --> 00:02:01.721
[bleep] dammit.

00:02:01.821 --> 00:02:03.389
And the allthread breaks.

00:02:03.489 --> 00:02:04.189
[groans]

00:02:04.256 --> 00:02:05.557
And that just snapped free.

00:02:05.624 --> 00:02:06.592
Yeah.

00:02:06.692 --> 00:02:07.594
I'm just floored.

00:02:07.694 --> 00:02:09.128
I don't know what
to do at this point

00:02:09.195 --> 00:02:12.097
except take it all back
apart and get some parts

00:02:12.164 --> 00:02:14.934
fixed back on it fast.

00:02:15.034 --> 00:02:17.237
I'm scared of this one.

00:02:17.337 --> 00:02:20.072
I'm really trying to make this
blade as smooth and beautiful

00:02:20.139 --> 00:02:21.040
as possible.

00:02:21.107 --> 00:02:22.475
Whoo-hoo.

00:02:22.575 --> 00:02:24.210
I've got to fix the warp
that these guys don't have.

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:26.078
They're already working
on handles and I'm not.

00:02:26.145 --> 00:02:27.947
So I've got to just hustle.

00:02:28.014 --> 00:02:29.515
In this competition,
especially,

00:02:29.616 --> 00:02:31.651
what people do is they
go and do the first round

00:02:31.717 --> 00:02:32.919
making the blade.

00:02:33.019 --> 00:02:34.086
When it comes to the
handle-making round,

00:02:34.186 --> 00:02:35.454
they're spending
so much time trying

00:02:35.555 --> 00:02:37.023
to fix the blade that
they'll forget the handle,

00:02:37.090 --> 00:02:38.958
and look-- you've become
like the handle mangler.

00:02:39.025 --> 00:02:41.494
I mean, how many kills have
you had based on the handle?

00:02:41.594 --> 00:02:43.229
It's in our testing
that it shows

00:02:43.296 --> 00:02:46.532
that if you don't do the handle
properly, it falls apart.

00:02:46.598 --> 00:02:50.303
I'm going to try clamping the
blade between an angle iron,

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:53.439
putting heat to it and torquing
on the spine where it's bent,

00:02:53.539 --> 00:02:54.807
start straightening it out.

00:02:54.908 --> 00:02:57.309
I'm keeping my edge in
the water cool so that way

00:02:57.376 --> 00:03:00.546
I do not lose the temper
in the edge of it.

00:03:00.612 --> 00:03:02.615
It took me way longer
to get the warp out

00:03:02.682 --> 00:03:03.382
than I thought it would.

00:03:03.449 --> 00:03:04.784
What is happening right now?

00:03:04.884 --> 00:03:06.185
Why is he so far behind?

00:03:06.252 --> 00:03:07.820
Well, he wasted
45 minutes trying

00:03:07.887 --> 00:03:09.121
to straighten that blade.

00:03:09.188 --> 00:03:11.290
And then there's been
a real persnickety

00:03:11.357 --> 00:03:12.625
fit up with the guard.

00:03:12.725 --> 00:03:14.427
And with this
amount of time left,

00:03:14.527 --> 00:03:16.496
it's good enough, good enough,
good enough, get it in there.

00:03:16.562 --> 00:03:18.297
Clock is worrying me
really bad right now.

00:03:18.364 --> 00:03:19.532
It's ticking down fast.

00:03:19.632 --> 00:03:21.534
I am way behind
the wall right now.

00:03:21.634 --> 00:03:23.002
I've got to get the
handle glued up,

00:03:23.069 --> 00:03:24.504
end capped, bring it
over to the grinder,

00:03:24.570 --> 00:03:26.005
and start refining everything.

00:03:26.072 --> 00:03:27.807
And then I've got
to get an edge.

00:03:27.874 --> 00:03:30.242
I'm just like, screw it,
I've got to help this guy.

00:03:30.342 --> 00:03:31.978
Come on, brother, you're
making me nervous.

00:03:32.045 --> 00:03:33.979
We've got Mark and
Scott trying to find

00:03:34.079 --> 00:03:36.916
the right tap for his blade.

00:03:36.983 --> 00:03:38.251
Right here, right here.
Here you go.

00:03:38.351 --> 00:03:39.619
I guess I'm just
the kind of person--

00:03:39.719 --> 00:03:40.686
Thank you.

00:03:40.786 --> 00:03:42.020
 --that that's just
my mentality is, just

00:03:42.087 --> 00:03:43.656
don't leave anybody behind.

00:03:43.756 --> 00:03:44.991
Check your threads.

00:03:45.091 --> 00:03:46.425
Is it different?
So check your--

00:03:46.492 --> 00:03:47.860
No, it's the same.
It's the same, it's the same.

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:49.462
OK.

00:03:49.562 --> 00:03:50.730
Now we've got Matt coming
over to throw in his two cents

00:03:50.797 --> 00:03:51.630
to give everybody a hand.

00:03:51.731 --> 00:03:53.032
That's awesome.

00:03:53.132 --> 00:03:55.167
So he's got the right-sized
hole with the right-sized tap.

00:03:55.234 --> 00:03:56.168
And now is the time.

00:03:56.235 --> 00:03:57.770
Don't cheat it by--

00:03:57.870 --> 00:03:58.738
Oh, man!

00:03:58.838 --> 00:03:59.772
See?

00:03:59.839 --> 00:04:01.440
The tap snapped off.

00:04:01.507 --> 00:04:03.208
- Come on.
- Oh, [bleep].

00:04:03.275 --> 00:04:04.510
What am I going to do?

00:04:04.577 --> 00:04:06.445
My first thought process
is, punch another hole.

00:04:06.512 --> 00:04:08.047
Get on that grinder
man, you can do it.

00:04:08.147 --> 00:04:09.915
1 minute, bladesmiths.

00:04:09.982 --> 00:04:11.416
I'm going as fast as I can.

00:04:11.517 --> 00:04:12.818
Make sure you get
that edge, dude.

00:04:12.919 --> 00:04:14.520
I need to etch this or
I'm going home right now,

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:15.654
because that's a parameter.

00:04:15.755 --> 00:04:16.723
Look at that.

00:04:16.823 --> 00:04:18.224
Matt getting ready
just to dunk it in.

00:04:18.324 --> 00:04:20.225
15 seconds.

00:04:20.292 --> 00:04:21.927
There you go, dude.
You've got this.

00:04:21.994 --> 00:04:22.928
10 seconds.

00:04:23.029 --> 00:04:23.929
Get in the etch.

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:25.164
There you go.

00:04:25.264 --> 00:04:26.598
Yes.
Yes.

00:04:26.698 --> 00:04:27.666
5.

00:04:27.733 --> 00:04:28.634
Yeah, buddy.

00:04:28.701 --> 00:04:29.835
4, 3, 2--

00:04:29.935 --> 00:04:31.404
- Good job, man.
- --1.

00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:32.538
Bladesmiths, stop
what you're doing.

00:04:32.638 --> 00:04:33.740
That's a heck of a comeback.

00:04:33.806 --> 00:04:35.674
Scott, pull your
blade out of that acid.

00:04:35.741 --> 00:04:38.911
This second round of
competition is over.

00:04:38.977 --> 00:04:40.146
[cheers, applause]

00:04:40.212 --> 00:04:41.947
Good comeback, my man.
Good comeback.

00:04:42.048 --> 00:04:43.149
Good job, man.

00:04:43.249 --> 00:04:44.317
Way to hang in there
till the end, man.

00:04:44.384 --> 00:04:45.518
Thank you, guys.

00:04:45.618 --> 00:04:47.086
If it wasn't for
the other smiths

00:04:47.186 --> 00:04:49.655
actually stepping in and helping
me, I wouldn't have made it.

00:04:49.756 --> 00:04:53.125
But inside, I'm really
pissed off because I know

00:04:53.191 --> 00:04:55.661
I could have done way better.

00:04:55.727 --> 00:04:58.197
Bladesmiths, welcome
to the strength test--

00:04:58.263 --> 00:05:01.433
the dreaded, dreaded rod chop.

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:03.502
To test the strength and
durability of your edge

00:05:03.602 --> 00:05:05.938
as well as the overall
construction of your blades,

00:05:06.005 --> 00:05:08.708
I'll be bashing them
into these threaded rods.

00:05:08.808 --> 00:05:10.376
We're going to use
this test to figure

00:05:10.476 --> 00:05:12.712
out who comes out unscathed
and who gets screwed.

00:05:12.779 --> 00:05:14.280
[laughter]
Matt, you're up first.

00:05:14.347 --> 00:05:15.281
You ready?

00:05:15.348 --> 00:05:16.282
Let's get it.

00:05:16.349 --> 00:05:19.552
[music playing]

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:28.627
Oh.

00:05:28.694 --> 00:05:29.695
[clatter]

00:05:29.762 --> 00:05:31.264
Nice.

00:05:31.364 --> 00:05:33.932
Well, Matt, you blades
are all still in one piece.

00:05:33.999 --> 00:05:39.672
But unfortunately, your edge
has split open to the point

00:05:39.772 --> 00:05:42.742
where there's a piece
of brass stuck inside.

00:05:42.842 --> 00:05:43.943
It is still straight.

00:05:44.043 --> 00:05:45.411
It is still in one piece.

00:05:45.511 --> 00:05:48.581
But there's no denying that
that's split in the seam

00:05:48.681 --> 00:05:49.615
right there.

00:05:49.682 --> 00:05:51.316
OK.

00:05:51.383 --> 00:05:52.485
Mark, you're seeing
what I'm up to.

00:05:52.585 --> 00:05:54.019
What are you thinking?

00:05:54.086 --> 00:05:54.987
Make it or break it.

00:05:55.054 --> 00:05:55.954
All right.

00:05:56.054 --> 00:05:57.055
One or the other.

00:05:57.155 --> 00:05:58.657
Oh, crap.
Oh, crap.

00:05:58.724 --> 00:05:59.659
Oh, crap.

00:05:59.726 --> 00:06:03.096
[music playing]

00:06:12.337 --> 00:06:13.639
Took out two brass rods, baby.

00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:14.473
Yeah, you did.

00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:15.941
First one right there.

00:06:16.008 --> 00:06:16.976
All right, Mark.

00:06:17.076 --> 00:06:18.244
Blade's still in one piece.

00:06:18.311 --> 00:06:19.878
It's still straight.

00:06:19.945 --> 00:06:23.649
There's definitely two areas
of chip out right here.

00:06:23.716 --> 00:06:26.319
And in between the chip
out, there's some roll.

00:06:26.419 --> 00:06:27.853
All being said, it
held up pretty well.

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:31.290
I mean, this is a brutal
test, and it really shows

00:06:31.390 --> 00:06:32.658
the strength of your blade.

00:06:32.758 --> 00:06:33.759
Well done.

00:06:33.826 --> 00:06:35.260
Right on.

00:06:35.360 --> 00:06:36.528
- Good job, bud.
- [exhales]

00:06:36.628 --> 00:06:37.930
All right, Scott.
You see me.

00:06:37.997 --> 00:06:38.931
You ready for this?

00:06:38.998 --> 00:06:39.932
Yeah, give her hell.

00:06:40.032 --> 00:06:41.800
All right.

00:06:41.901 --> 00:06:45.471
[music playing]

00:06:49.174 --> 00:06:53.179
Holy sh-- [laughs] [whistles]

00:06:54.112 --> 00:06:55.614
All right, Scott.

00:06:55.681 --> 00:06:59.452
For a beefy chef's knife, this
thing held up really well.

00:06:59.518 --> 00:07:01.287
Your blade is still straight.

00:07:01.353 --> 00:07:04.757
The edge took some
flatting in spots.

00:07:04.824 --> 00:07:06.259
It's not chipped,
it's not rolled.

00:07:06.325 --> 00:07:08.194
It's just kind of mushed
in by a little bit.

00:07:08.294 --> 00:07:09.428
I think you knocked
it out of the park.

00:07:09.528 --> 00:07:11.397
- Thank you.
- Nice job, dude.

00:07:11.463 --> 00:07:12.698
- Thanks, man.
- Good job.

00:07:12.798 --> 00:07:14.099
- Thank you.
- All right, guys.

00:07:14.166 --> 00:07:15.134
I give you to Doug.

00:07:15.201 --> 00:07:17.135
Oh, yeah.

00:07:17.202 --> 00:07:18.537
All right, bladesmiths.

00:07:18.604 --> 00:07:22.508
This is the sharpness test,
the fabric loom slice.

00:07:22.575 --> 00:07:25.044
Now, a sharp blade should
cut through the yarn.

00:07:25.111 --> 00:07:28.547
A dull edge or a jagged
edge, we just pull at it.

00:07:28.613 --> 00:07:30.282
Matt, you're up first.
You ready for this?

00:07:30.382 --> 00:07:31.584
Let it rip.

00:07:44.764 --> 00:07:48.300
That tip had such
an edge on it, bro.

00:07:48.367 --> 00:07:49.501
All right, Matt.

00:07:49.601 --> 00:07:51.904
First up, the front
part here is sharp.

00:07:52.004 --> 00:07:54.940
In the first cut, it
cut the yarn nicely.

00:07:55.007 --> 00:07:58.711
On the drag slice,
it did cut initially.

00:07:58.811 --> 00:08:01.180
But then on the third
one, where you're really

00:08:01.247 --> 00:08:03.281
exposing it to this
particular edge,

00:08:03.348 --> 00:08:04.784
it just dragged through it.

00:08:04.850 --> 00:08:07.419
But overall, sir, it will cut.

00:08:07.519 --> 00:08:08.988
- Yeah.
- Fantastic.

00:08:09.088 --> 00:08:10.089
Good job, man.
Good job.

00:08:10.189 --> 00:08:11.223
All right, Mark.
Your turn.

00:08:11.323 --> 00:08:12.191
So are you ready?

00:08:12.291 --> 00:08:13.158
Let her cut.

00:08:13.259 --> 00:08:14.260
Let's do that.

00:08:14.327 --> 00:08:17.897
[music playing]

00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:28.373
Ooh.

00:08:28.473 --> 00:08:32.878
[laughs] [exhales]

00:08:32.945 --> 00:08:34.547
Good job.

00:08:34.614 --> 00:08:35.981
All right, Mark.

00:08:36.048 --> 00:08:36.983
The front is sharp.

00:08:37.049 --> 00:08:39.018
Even parts of the
blade opens it up.

00:08:39.085 --> 00:08:41.186
It cuts easily.

00:08:41.253 --> 00:08:42.154
It will cut.

00:08:42.254 --> 00:08:43.555
Yes.

00:08:43.622 --> 00:08:45.124
All right, Scott.
It's your turn, sir.

00:08:45.191 --> 00:08:46.458
You ready?
- Yep.

00:08:46.559 --> 00:08:47.793
Let's do this.

00:08:47.894 --> 00:08:51.264
[music playing]

00:08:57.603 --> 00:08:58.804
Did it cut?

00:09:04.576 --> 00:09:05.611
No, it's not cutting.

00:09:05.711 --> 00:09:06.645
I think it's pulling.

00:09:06.745 --> 00:09:07.679
All right, Scott.

00:09:07.746 --> 00:09:09.115
Let's talk about
your blade here.

00:09:09.181 --> 00:09:13.085
Your edge, it's not very sharp.

00:09:13.185 --> 00:09:15.687
It did cut a little bit.

00:09:15.787 --> 00:09:18.691
On the draw slice, slightest.

00:09:18.757 --> 00:09:20.759
It even pulled it
all the way down.

00:09:20.826 --> 00:09:24.797
Your blade, sir,
will cut a little.

00:09:24.864 --> 00:09:25.564
Thank you.

00:09:25.664 --> 00:09:26.899
All right, gentlemen.

00:09:26.966 --> 00:09:29.068
Bladesmith leaving
the forge is--

00:09:32.938 --> 00:09:33.672
Matt.

00:09:33.738 --> 00:09:35.508
Your blade didn't make the cut.

00:09:35.608 --> 00:09:37.576
Well, Matt, your blade
suffered some serious damage

00:09:37.676 --> 00:09:38.944
on the strength test.

00:09:39.010 --> 00:09:40.846
It's literally splitting
in half at the edge.

00:09:40.913 --> 00:09:43.449
And that in turn impaired
its ability to cut

00:09:43.549 --> 00:09:44.784
well on the sharpness test.

00:09:44.884 --> 00:09:46.418
And it's for that reason
we're sending you home.

00:09:46.485 --> 00:09:47.385
Yeah.

00:09:47.486 --> 00:09:48.621
I understand.

00:09:48.721 --> 00:09:51.022
Matt, please
surrender your blade.

00:09:51.123 --> 00:09:52.925
Right now, I feel
disappointed I didn't make it.

00:09:53.025 --> 00:09:55.027
But at the same time,
I feel great because I

00:09:55.094 --> 00:09:56.395
did make it this far.

00:09:56.462 --> 00:09:58.563
I think my blade actually ended
up splitting because I didn't

00:09:58.630 --> 00:10:00.166
clean my needles
and bolts, and those

00:10:00.232 --> 00:10:02.301
didn't weld together
good enough and set me

00:10:02.367 --> 00:10:04.603
up ultimately for this failure.

00:10:04.703 --> 00:10:05.937
When I get home and
fire up my press,

00:10:06.037 --> 00:10:07.573
canister is going to
be the very first thing

00:10:07.639 --> 00:10:09.241
on my list to start working on.

00:10:09.308 --> 00:10:11.344
[music playing]

